 ## Overview
-Volume Illustration Tone Shading is a non-photorealistic illumination model for volume rendering that combines gaseous illumination with directed shading based on the signed dot product between the volume sample gradient and the light vector [S1]. Unlike standard Gooch shading, the illuminated object contribution is zero at orientations orthogonal to the light vector, matching diffuse illumination models [S1].
+Volume Illustration Tone Shading is a non-photorealistic illumination model that enhances shape perception in volume rendering by mapping the orientation of volume samples relative to a light source to a warm-to-cool color gradient [S1][S2, unofficial]. It distinguishes itself from standard Gooch shading by modifying the illuminated object contribution to use only the positive dot product, ensuring the contribution becomes zero at orientations orthogonal to the light vector, thereby matching familiar diffuse illumination models more closely [S2, unofficial][S3, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
-**Inputs and Outputs:**
-- **Inputs:** Volume dataset $V$ with scalar values; gradient field $\nabla f_n$ (normalized volume sample gradient) [S1]; light vectors $L_i$ (unit vectors, $i=1 \dots NL$) [S1]; illuminated object color $I_i$ (from transfer function) [S1]; gaseous illumination color $I_G$ (from transfer function) [S1]; parameters $k_{ta}, k_{td}, k_{ty}, k_{tb}$ [S1].
-- **Output:** Final color $c$ for each volume sample [S1].
